// The three pressure doors in the Waterbirth Island Dungeon, on the way to the
// Dagannoth Kings.
//
// Each door is a `width=3` loc standing on three `dagannoth_pressure_door_floor`
// tiles, with a pressure pad at either end on its west side:
// `dagannoth_pressure_pad_2` level with the door's own tile and
// `dagannoth_pressure_pad_1` two tiles north of it. Both pads must be weighted
// at the same time for the door to open -- which is the whole puzzle, because
// one player cannot stand on two pads.
//
// The intended solutions, and both are supported here:
//   * Two players, one on each pad.
//   * Alone: drop something on one pad and stand on the other. The pads are
//     adjacent to the door, so a player standing on one is already in operable
//     distance and can click the door without stepping off it.
//
// Sources. The mechanic is not implemented in any of our references -- void-634
// carries only the *other* barrier system in this dungeon (`DoorSupport.kt`, the
// `Destroy`-op npcs 2440/2443/2446 that drop `dagcave_invisibleblocking`), and
// majula 225-289 has nothing here at all. The loc set and its states come from
// `ref/rs3/config/dump.loc` (8957-8965), which is also where the canonical names
// used below come from; the two-players-or-an-item rule is from the RuneScape
// and OSRS wikis: "To open a door, one player must stand on a button while their
// companion stands on the other", with the pet rock or a dropped item standing
// in for the second player. Graded C in the ledger: sourced and reasoned, but
// never observed against a 2011 server.
//
// The animation is in the data, not in this script, and that is the whole
// reason the state names look redundant:
//
//   seq_2348  frames f2 -> f15 ascending, loopcount=1   the door LOWERING
//   seq_2349  50-tick hold on f15, then f15 -> f2       the door RISING
//   seq_2350  single frame f15, looping                 held down, static
//
// So `dagannoth_pressure_opening2` (anim 2348, blockwalk=no) *is* the door going
// down, and it finishes on f15 -- the same pose `dagannoth_pressure_open` holds
// statically. The closed door carries 2349, so reverting to it plays the door
// rising back up after its own built-in pause.
//
// That makes one `loc_change` enough for the whole cycle: swap to `opening2` and
// the door lowers and stays down and passable; when the change lapses it reverts
// to closed, which raises itself. No queued script, no hand-timed chain, and
// both animations play because the locs were authored to do it.
//
// An earlier version here went straight to `dagannoth_pressure_open` and called
// the intermediate states cosmetic. They are not: skipping to the static pose
// teleports the door into the floor with no movement at all.
// `dagannoth_pressure_opening` (anim 2350 but blockwalk=yes) stays unused --
// static-down yet still solid, which fits a closing phase rather than an
// opening one.

// How long the door stays open, in ticks. The wikis say the barriers in this
// dungeon "eventually" reset rather than giving a figure; 25 ticks is fifteen
// seconds, comfortably enough to walk through and consistent with the 30s the
// door supports use in void.
[proc,dag_door_open_ticks]()(int)
return(25);

// True if something is holding this pad down: a player standing on it, or a pet
// rock dropped on it.
//
// The pet rock is a *specific* item, not a stand-in for "anything heavy". The
// era sources agree on this and on the reason: the rock "substitutes as a
// player", so the pad is weighed by an entity rather than by inventory clutter.
// The 2009scape emulator carries the same rule (merge request 712, "Pet rocks on
// pressure pads"), and the RuneScape wiki says the rock is obtained from
// Askeladden in Rellekka precisely so a solo player can open these doors.
//
// The modern OSRS wiki says any dropped item works. That is a later divergence
// and is wrong for 2011 -- taking it would have made the puzzle trivial, since
// any player arrives carrying something.
//
// The RuneScape wiki also names "the egg from Scrambled!" alongside the rock.
// Deliberately not implemented: the only matching obj in our data is 7078
// `bowl_egg_scrambled`, which is the food rather than a pet, and whether that
// quest existed by May 2011 is unconfirmed. Better a missing alternative than an
// invented one.
//
// Players are found with `huntall` -- the op Jagex had for this, opcode 2031,
// confirmed and already used in our content. The engine also carries a
// `PLAYER_FINDALLZONE` that would do the same job, but its opcode (2146) is
// marked TODO in `rs-pack/src/cache/script.rs`, a guess rather than a verified
// number, and the VM notes it duplicates `huntall`. Exposing the guessed one
// would add an invented op to the emulated surface for no gain.
//
// `.huntnext` puts each result in the *secondary* slot so the acting player
// stays primary; the trigger still needs them for the loc it is operating on.
[proc,dag_pad_weighted](coord $pad)(boolean)
huntall($pad, 1, 0);
while (.huntnext = true) {
    if (.coord = $pad) {
        return(true);
    }
}
if (obj_find($pad, pet_rock) = true) {
    return(true);
}
return(false);

// The pads sit west of the door: one level with its south tile, one two north.
// All three doors are placed at the same rotation, so these offsets are read off
// the placements rather than derived from `loc_angle` -- if a fourth is ever
// added at another rotation this is the line to revisit.
[proc,dag_door_pads](coord $door)(coord, coord)
return(movecoord($door, -1, 0, 0), movecoord($door, -1, 0, 2));

[oploc1,_dagannoth_pressure_door]
def_coord $door = loc_coord;
def_coord $south;
def_coord $north;
$south, $north = ~dag_door_pads($door);

def_boolean $south_down = ~dag_pad_weighted($south);
def_boolean $north_down = ~dag_pad_weighted($north);

if ($south_down = false | $north_down = false) {
    // Deliberately silent.
    //
    // No message text for this door survives in anything we can read: the cache
    // carries configs only, `ref/rs3/config` has no strings for it, and neither
    // wiki quotes one. Writing a line here would be inventing 2011 dialogue, and
    // a wrong string is worse than none -- it reads as authentic and never gets
    // questioned. The pads are visible on the floor at either end, which is how
    // the puzzle is meant to be read anyway.
    //
    // If a log or a video from the era ever turns up with the real wording, this
    // is the spot for it.
    return;
}

loc_change(loc_param(next_loc_stage), ~dag_door_open_ticks);
